h now (using your usual method).  \n  2. If possible, consider an water‑saving technique such as **skip‑furrow** or **drip** to reduce total water use while meeting the crop’s demand.\n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  1. Re‑measure soil moisture 24 hours after irrigation; aim for a reading that does not drop below the current **16.0 %** before the next scheduled irrigation.  \n  2. Keep the irrigation interval aligned with the phase‑wise schedule (≈ 7 days for early growth, ≈ 10 days for tillering, ≈ 7 days for grand growth, ≈ 15 days for maturity) and adjust if rainfall occurs.  \n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  1. If soil moisture remains below **10 %** after the irrigation or the crop shows wilting, yellowing, or reduced growth.  \n  2. If you notice persistent high humidity (≥ 88 %) combined with disease symptoms (e.g., leaf spots), request an on‑site agronomist visit.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 1.49, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 190, "completion_tokens": 36, "seconds": 0.6, "status": "ok", "seq": 6}, {"purpose": "answer", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "prompt_tokens": 1914, "completion_tokens": 900, "seconds": 2.44, "status": "ok", "seq": 7}, {"purpose": "fact_check", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 1257, "completion_tokens": 1019, "seconds": 1.49, "status": "ok", "seq": 8}]}
