: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: farm (about this farm now).\n- Use the farm's sensor readings, alerts and history as the Observed Facts, and AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE for what they mean.\n- If the readings are missing, old or not enough to answer, still say what they do show, then name exactly what is missing (for example a soil-moisture reading, the crop stage, a description of the symptom). Use Confidence Low in that case. Do not refuse outright.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (general)\nAnswer the farmer's question from the knowledge text, using this farm's data where it is relevant.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-10-05 08:22 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.9716, 77.5946\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-10-05 08:22 UTC\n- Temperature: 30.5 C\n- Humidity: 88.0 %\n- Soil moisture: 16.0 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nTrends (compare current vs earlier observation):\n- Soil moisture decreased by 29.0% compared to the previous observation (45.0% -> 16.0%).\n- Humidity increased by 28.0% compared to the previous observation (60.0% -> 88.0%).\n- Temperature increased by 3.5C compared to the previous observation (27.0C -> 30.5C).\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [warning] Unusual sensor pattern for this field (FlyBrain score 0.64 > threshold 0.39, synthetic graph)\n\n# SENSOR PATTERN CHECK (FlyBrain)\nThe latest sensor reading scored 0.64 on the FlyBrain pattern check, above this farm's own normal threshold of 0.39: its combination of temperature, humidity and soil moisture is unusual for this field. This is a statistical flag from a synthetic test graph (not the real fly connectome) simulation, not a diagnosis.\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025: Sugarcane (Current Season Practices) — Main Information:\n- **Gap filling**: any gap filling with healthy setts should be completed early in June to maintain uniform crop stand.\n- **Nitrogen top-dressing**: second split of nitrogen (approximately 60–75 kg N/ha) using urea should be applied before rains or irrigation, following the previously applied basal dose.\n- **Zinc deficiency**: in zinc-deficient fields, apply 25 kg zinc sulphate/ha, or spray 0.5% ZnSO₄ solution on the standing crop.\n- **Intercropping**: short-duration pulses (moong, urd) intercropped during early monsoon are recommended to improve soil fertility and farmer income.\n- **Weeding/hoeing**: should be completed by July to avoid competition during the peak growth phase.\n- **Pest surveillance from June onward**:\n  - *Top borer*: common in this period; apply Chlorantraniliprole 0.4% GR @ 10 kg/ha in leaf whorls during early July.\n\nPradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY): Crop Insurance for Farmers — Main Information:\n- **Premium structure**: the maximum premium payable by the farmer is capped at 2% of the sum insured for Kharif food and oilseed crops, 1.5% for Rabi food and oilseed crops, and 5% for annual commercial/horticultural crops (sugarcane, being a commercial crop, generally falls in the higher premium-rate category, though the government subsidizes the remainder of the actuarial premium). The remaining premium above the farmer's capped share is subsidized by the government.\n- **Risks covered**:\n  1. *Yield losses (standing crop)*: coverage for non-preventable risks including natural fire and lightning, storm, hailstorm, cyclone, typhoon, tempest, hurricane, tornado, flood, inundation, landslide, drought, dry spells, and pest/disease attack.\n  2. *Prevented sowing*: if adverse weather prevents planting, farmers can claim indemnity up to a maximum of 25% of the sum insured.\n\nPradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY): Crop Insurance for Farmers — Main Information:\n3. *Post-harvest losses*: coverage on an individual-farm basis for crops in \"cut and spread\" condition, for up to 14 days (maximum) from harvesting.\n  4. *Localised calamities*: individual-farm-basis coverage for hailstorm, landslide, and inundation affecting scattered farmlands within a notified area.\n- **Eligibility**: all farmers, including sharecroppers and tenant farmers growing notified crops in notified areas, are eligible. The scheme is voluntary; non-loanee farmers' share of total coverage rose to 55% in 2023-24, reflecting growing voluntary uptake.\n\nSugarcane Nutrient/Fertilizer Management — Main Information:\n- Nutrient removal by a 100 t/ha crop: 208 kg N, 53 kg P, 280 kg K, 30 kg S, 3.4 kg Fe, 1.2 kg Mn, 0.6 kg Cu.\n- Regional nitrogen requirement differs sharply: **northern India needs about 150–180 kg N/ha, while southern India (including Karnataka) needs 250–350 kg N/ha.**\n- If soil test values are unavailable, apply 60–80 kg P₂O₅ and 60 kg K₂O/ha in north India, versus **100 kg P₂O₅/ha and 80–120 kg K₂O/ha in southern India.**\n- **Zone-wise recommended fertilizer dose relevant to Karnataka (Peninsular Zone)**: Plant crop — 250 kg N, 100 kg P₂O₅, 125 kg K₂O per hectare; Ratoon crop — 200 kg N, 60 kg P₂O₅, 60 kg K₂O per hectare.\n- Application of well-decomposed FYM/compost at 10–15 t/ha, or composted press mud at 5 t/ha, is recommended as a base organic supplement.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: Should I irrigate?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"29.0 %\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"45.0 %\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"3.5 °C\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); +10. **Observation** - Soil moisture: **16.0 %** ...\nFarmer: Any disease?\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"29.0 %\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"45.0 %\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"28.0 %\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); +4. **Observation** - Temperature: **30.5 °C** (current ...\nFarmer: Compare with yesterday.\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"27.0 °C\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"3.5 °C\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"60.0 %\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); +7. **Observation** - Current temperature: **30.5 °C** - ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?", "farmer": "ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?", "sarvam": "How is my crop?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8141, "sarvam": 0.8524}, "retrieved": [{"source": "sugarcane/06_icar_kharif_agro_advisory_2025_sugarcane.md", "section": "ICAR Kharif Agro-Advisory 2025: Sugarcane (Current Season Practices) — Main Information", "score": 0.8524, "via": "sarvam"}, {"source": "insurance/01_pradhan_mantri_fasal_bima_yojana_pmfby.md", "section": "Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY): Crop Insurance for Farmers — Main Information", "score": 0.8521, "via": "sarvam"}, {"source": "insurance/01_pradhan_mantri_fasal_bima_yojana_pmfby.md", "section": "Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY): Crop Insurance for Farmers — Main Information", "score": 0.8489, "via": "sarvam"}, {"source": "fertilizer/01_fertilizer_and_nutrient_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Nutrient/Fertilizer Management — Main Information", "score": 0.8485, "via": "sarvam"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- Temperature: **30.5 °C** (current reading)  \n- Humidity: **88 %** (current reading)  \n- Soil moisture: **16 %** (current reading)  \n- Trend: soil moisture decreased from **45 %** to **16 %** (‑29 %); humidity increased from **60 %** to **88 %** (+28 %); temperature increased from **27 °C** to **30.5 °C** (+3.5 °C)  \n- Active alert: **[warning] Unusual sensor pattern** (FlyBrain score **0.64** > threshold **0.39**)  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- The sharp drop in soil‑moisture to **16 %** together with high temperature (**30.5 °C**) indicates the crop is likely experiencing water stress.  \n- The rise in humidity to **88 %** can create a favourable environment for foliar diseases, especially if the canopy stays wet.  \n- The statistical alert suggests this combination of high temperature, high humidity and low soil moisture is atypical for your field; it may reflect a real stress condition or a sensor anomaly that needs verification.  \n\n**Confidence** – **Medium** – because we have reliable sensor data but lack crop‑stage information and visual symptom reports.  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. Verify the soil‑moisture sensor reading (check probe placement, ensure good soil contact).  \n2. If the reading is correct, apply irrigation to raise soil moisture to a level that prevents wilting (aim for a noticeable increase from **16 %**).  \n3. Inspect the crop for any early signs of leaf yellowing, wilting, or fungal growth, especially on lower leaves where high humidity can promote disease.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Record soil‑moisture daily for the next 3–5 days; look for a trend upward after irrigation.  \n- Keep an eye on leaf colour and turgor; note any new spots, lesions, or excessive moisture on the canopy.  \n- Re‑check the temperature and humidity sensors each morning to confirm they remain consistent.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If soil moisture remains at or below **16 %** despite irrigation attempts.  \n- If you observe any disease symptoms (e.g., leaf spots, white mould, wilting not relieved by water).  \n- If the sensor‑pattern warning persists after sensor verification, indicating a possible underlying issue that needs agronomic diagnosis.", "claims": [], "unsupported": ["number: 45 %", "number: 29 %", "number: 60 %", "number: 28 %", "number: 27 °C", "number: 3.5 °C", "number: 3–5 days", "judgement: 16 % high"], "final_answer": "ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"45 %\" (ಈ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"29 %\" (ಈ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); \"60 %\" (ಈ ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ); +5.\n\n**Observation**  \n- Temperature: **30.5 °C** (current reading)  \n- Humidity: **88 %** (current reading)  \n- Soil moisture: **16 %** (current reading)  \n- Trend: soil moisture decreased from **45 %** to **16 %** (‑29 %); humidity increased from **60 %** to **88 %** (+28 %); temperature increased from **27 °C** to **30.5 °C** (+3.5 °C)  \n- Active alert: **[warning] Unusual sensor pattern** (FlyBrain score **0.64** > threshold **0.39**)  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- The sharp drop in soil‑moisture to **16 %** together with high temperature (**30.5 °C**) indicates the crop is likely experiencing water stress.  \n- The rise in humidity to **88 %** can create a favourable environment for foliar diseases, especially if the canopy stays wet.  \n- The statistical alert suggests this combination of high temperature, high humidity and low soil moisture is atypical for your field; it may reflect a real stress condition or a sensor anomaly that needs verification.  \n\n**Confidence** – **Medium** – because we have reliable sensor data but lack crop‑stage information and visual symptom reports.  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. Verify the soil‑moisture sensor reading (check probe placement, ensure good soil contact).  \n2. If the reading is correct, apply irrigation to raise soil moisture to a level that prevents wilting (aim for a noticeable increase from **16 %**).  \n3. Inspect the crop for any early signs of leaf yellowing, wilting, or fungal growth, especially on lower leaves where high humidity can promote disease.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Record soil‑moisture daily for the next 3–5 days; look for a trend upward after irrigation.  \n- Keep an eye on leaf colour and turgor; note any new spots, lesions, or excessive moisture on the canopy.  \n- Re‑check the temperature and humidity sensors each morning to confirm they remain consistent.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If soil moisture remains at or below **16 %** despite irrigation attempts.  \n- If you observe any disease symptoms (e.g., leaf spots, white mould, wilting not relieved by water).  \n- If the sensor‑pattern warning persists after sensor verification, indicating a possible underlying issue that needs agronomic diagnosis.\n\n(ಈ ಉತ್ತರದ ಪೂರ್ಣ ಪರಿಶೀಲನೆ ಈಗ ಲಭ್ಯವಿಲ್ಲ; ಸಂಖ್ಯೆ ಮತ್ತು ಹೆಸರು ಪರಿಶೀಲನೆ ಮಾತ್ರ ನಡೆದಿದೆ.)", "fact_check": "skipped", "fact_check_seconds": 0.0, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": null, "completion_tokens": null, "seconds": 0.0, "status": "skipped", "seq": 15}, {"purpose": "answer", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "prompt_tokens": null, "completion_tokens": null, "seconds": 0.49, "status": "rate_limited", "seq": 16}, {"purpose": "answer", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "prompt_tokens": 2197, "completion_tokens": 917, "seconds": 3.17, "status": "ok", "seq": 17}, {"purpose": "fact_check", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": null, "completion_tokens": null, "seconds": 0.0, "status": "skipped", "seq": 18}]}
